editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:The line did not seem to bend, and that was the strangest part of the morning. He walked along a cut that ran dead straight before him, for as far as he could see, and there was a floor on his right hand the whole way. Yet when he looked back, at the twentieth length, an old man on a box was no longer behind him. He was behind and to the right, and at the fortieth he was farther round; the straight line was carrying them about a middle that none of them had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:107:Cael knew it by the tally and by nothing else. He looked across, and there was the whole of that floor between, holding the afternoon; and on the far side of it, on the rim, there was a mark no bigger than a seed. He supposed that it was an old man on a box, though he could not have told a mule from a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:119:The sun went over, and the water in Brom's skin went down. The fourth station was behind them, and the fifth, and at each of the holes she had done a thing that he did not at first understand. She had made Lira and Seln draw one length of the cord dead straight between two points of the cut. Then she had laid the slip of ash across, at the second knot, from the taut hemp to the lip, and had marked the slip with her thumbnail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:121:"What's that for?" Brom asked her, at the sixth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:127:The mark on the rim grew. It became a box, with a brown coat upon it, and an upright stick, and there were people about it now, and a mule. The old man had not shifted. Cael could see, at two hundred yards, that he was sitting exactly as he had been left, with both his hands on the ash and his face to the north, like a post that somebody has driven and gone away from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:147:"Fifty cords," said Brom. "About."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"How can you tell that?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:179:"That is an accident, and nobody means a thing like that. A hand went wrong, a very long time ago, and left it." She had two sheets on her knee already, which Lira had fetched out of the middle box at a word: one that she had drawn in a ravine three days before, and one from under a marsh. "It is on the west pier, in the second course. It is on this, from the hall. I drew it both times, and took it for part of the sign. It is cut there as carefully as everything else, to the same depth as everything else, at the same slant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:185:[[TIER-B: "The others are copies." — the packet gives the three words after "this is the first cut" and pins no speaker. Staged here: at the western stump, a hand outside the cut, after Karis has shown the copied slip of the tool on two of her own sheets and said that one stratum lay under all that she had; the conclusion itself is left for this line, and I have not written it in other words. Karis is the natural owner; Lira or Brom could say it to her. The next paragraph stands for any of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:189:She had the hide out a little later, on the flat top of a box, with the clean sheet pinned to its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:281:Brom went next, with his wrist asked for and held, and came to a stop a stride inside, beside the first stump. He did not come back at once; he looked down at the thing, and then he put his palm on the top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:303:The old man had got up from his box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-58.md:337:Cael saw it take him. The heavy shoulders went back, as though somebody had drawn a pack off them from behind; and the left hand came up, slowly, and closed upon the right wrist, where there was linen, and a slat, and under those a joint that had paid for everything that he had ever done. He stood so for the length of three breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:Karis had lent him her lamp. She sat across the fire with the hide rolled on her knees, and she was not reading; she was looking at the pale beyond the rim, as she had looked at it for most of an hour. Brom lay on his back, and Lira was a yard off, on her side, with her eyes open. On the far side of the fire three people and an old woman had lain down with their feet to the west. Nobody asked him what he put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:33:He had thought, on a shelf, with his forehead on the rock, that he had perhaps paid a little of it, and he had gone over that since. He would not have taken such a payment from another person; and Brom, who priced things, had told him behind a step of shale to pay when it could be paid, and not a day sooner, and not to ease his own back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:87:"Then lie down," said Brom, from his back. "It's what the rest of us are trying to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:97:Across the dead side of the fire somebody had got up. It was the tall man. He had a cup of his own on the embers, and a strip of linen, and he wrung the one out of the other with his big chapped hands, a little less hard than he had the night before; and he carried it on both palms to where a captain lay with his arm along a slat. Cael did not watch the rest. But he marked that there had been a second night of it, and he thought that there would be a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:109:Brom went across to them first, since he had the contract by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:Brom nodded, slowly, and went and stood by his box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:123:He stopped in front of the old man, who was sitting upon a box with the ash across his knees. They had sat side by side at one fire, those two, like a pair of gateposts set too close; they had walked at the tail of the same file for two days, and had stopped within a breath of one another at every halt, and had never once turned their heads. The broad man looked down at a bound knee. The old man looked up at a bound shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:She had done it herself. Three boxes stood in a row upon the rock, with the flat on top of them and the sack of meal beside, and there was only a pannier left upon that brown back, and a roll of canvas.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:179:She went along them, before she would get up, as she had gone along six people at a fork-stone. She looked under the linen on Brom's forearm, having asked, and told him that he was to keep it out of the wet and that the colours on his chest were coming on nicely. She took Karis's left hand, by her leave, and turned it to the light. She told Lira nothing whatever, since there was nothing that wanted telling, and Lira said that she had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:187:Her two hands went round the linen, above the laths and below. It did not take her long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:195:She came to Cael last. She did not ask to read him, since she had done that at the cut, in the dark, and had her finding. She looked at the tape through his shirt, and at the box which stood nearest to his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:203:[[TIER-B: Oryn's departure — "The day I have to." / "Noted," / "Everybody says that." … "They'll carry. They'll hurt," … "After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say." — Tier A 22 heads the unit "Oryn:" and elides twice; the "Noted," between her first and third lines is not pinned. Staged here: dawn of the 308th at the rim, the mule unloaded, three boxes on the rock at Cael's feet; her first line answers his question above about the leaf (she has still not heard it); "Noted," reads as his; "They'll carry. They'll hurt," is said of his ribs with her eyes on the box that he must take up; the last line is her term, as in her letter. If "The day I have to." answers something else, my question above wants turning. The paragraph below has her go — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:207:Cael had never seen her ride. She did it from a box, heavily, with a grunt, and sat sideways with her feet hanging and the rope in her fist, like a woman on a wall; and the mule, which had carried a researcher's whole archive across a country without complaint, laid back both ears at a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:217:Brom had put his hand to the lashing of his box. There was a bowl under it, of the mill's turning, which he had carried empty from a fork to a town and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:227:So there were three boxes on the rock, and nothing with hooves to put them on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:229:Brom laid his palm on the heaviest. He tried its weight with the one arm, and left it standing for the moment, and looked at the other two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:243:"She said both." He got the third up, and it was the reserve's box, which was the lightest; and they did hurt, in two straight lines, and they carried. "I'm entering that you were told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:He slung it above his sound leg, and came across the rock, at his own beat, and stopped a stride behind a big man with a box, where he had stood since a ford. He looked at nobody. He had asked nothing of any of them, that morning or on any other, and nobody had asked a thing of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:257:Cael saw Brom's head turn, by the width of a finger, and turn back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:263:Cael looked at them. There was a quiet man, and a girl with a box on her back, and a big one with his arm in linen. There was a small pale woman who had been right, and an old man on a stick, with a surveyor's case. He made the sixth himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-60.md:273:"West," said Brom, and picked up the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:19:Brom had been watching it with his jaw shut. His own left forearm was wound in linen for the whole of its length, and the bolt was out of it; a healer had seen to that under the southern wall, when she had finished with a shoulder. He went up onto the flat, heavily, and stopped a yard from a captain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:29:It was the only thing that they had been given leave to do, and they did it for the better part of an hour. Brom took two at a time in the crook of his right arm. Lira went farthest down the bed, where the big ones lay, and Seln brought small flat ones that would lock. Karis carried them one by one against her chest, since she was not to lean on a hand; and every stone that came up was taken at the edge of the flat by the tall man or the old woman, and set by them, and by nobody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:61:"There is a mule on the far side of the heap," said Seln. "She has three boxes on her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:It had told him what such places generally told him. There were nine heaps of bedding under the spilled heap, and the place for a tenth, swept and never lain on. There was a kettle. There were two bundles of bolts, with never a thread of colour upon a feather, and a second maul. He had turned over every blanket that he could reach, and there was not a scrap of paper in the whole of it, or a mark burned into a stave, or a name scratched on a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:155:She stood on three legs, with the fourth hoof tipped, and her nose in a bag that had been empty for some hours. The three boxes were on her back, and the sack of meal, and a healer's pannier. Not a lashing had shifted. She was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:185:Brom laid seven bowls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:191:Nobody was laying that side. The tall man sat with his forearms on his knees and his hands hanging, and the broad man was propped against a pack with his eyes shut. Teague stood beyond them at the edge of the light, looking east, and the lean old woman was at his arm with her board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:195:Brom got up. He went round the fire, heavily, to the place where a sack of turned bowls lay by a stranger's kettle; and he took out four, and set them along the far side, a yard apart, as the boy had set them. Nobody stopped him. The tall man's eyes followed his hand from one to the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:199:Brom had it out before he saw what he was doing. He stood holding it. It was of a different turning from his own, darker, with a chip in the rim; and Cael saw that he did not know where on that rock a person was to put such a thing down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:205:Brom came round the fire again, and sat down in his own place, and looked for a while at his two hands as though one of them had done something without asking the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:223:Karis had the three boxes off the mule and had counted every lashing. Beside her the old man sat propped against the middle one, with a blanket over his legs; and Lira was on her heels before him with a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:233:It was the tall man who brought it. He had begged a strip of linen from the old woman's roll, and heated water in his own cup, since there was no kettle to spare; and he wrung the thing out with those big chapped hands until it had nearly left off steaming. He had wrung it too hard. He carried it round to his captain upon both palms, as it had always been carried, and stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-56.md:249:"I've had a shoulder and a forearm since noon, and a knee to look at. The knee's held; he sat, and it never took a pound. I've been too spent to see past the end of my own hands." She looked down at him. "You carried stone for an hour with one arm. You've sat since with the other across you, and you've not once breathed to the bottom. I taped three of those myself, and I know what they sound like when they're let alone. Up, if you can get up. I don't put my hands in anybody at a fire with ten people looking on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:5:It came at the count of twenty-three, and all three bolts went to the one place: low down, by the mouth, at an old man who sat on the stones with his back to them. Cael heard them strike. They broke a yard above the grey head, where the lean of the thing was lowest, and the pieces skittered off behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:In the middle, where the lean stood well over their heads, the healer had Brom by the elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:She broke the shaft between her two hands, close to the skin, and Brom looked at the roof while she did it. The lean old woman had a strip of linen round the place before the stub had stopped moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:37:It was a single dull knock, like a post being driven; and then the three bows went, together, and he never learned where those bolts fell, since the hill had begun to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:His bolt struck the rock of the lip, a hand below a hood's knee. All three of them went flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:127:He did it with the one arm that he had, lying on his face, with a bolt in his shoulder and a shin on a board. He got his fist into the collar of that coat and dragged, and dragged again, until he had brought the boy back the single stride that the boy had gone out for him. Then he lay still, with his fist where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:185:Brom had come up the wedge. There was a hand of broken bolt standing out of his left forearm, with linen round it, and he was the colour of slate to the hair. He looked once at what lay at the roof's edge. He did not say anything. He walked out past it, into the open mouth of the shelter, and turned his face up to the shelf, and stood there like a door that has been shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:"That's three," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:225:It was moving. It was a thing moving which ought to be stopped; a captain had taught him the name for that on a ridge, with his arm in linen. The top of the heap had begun to lean out over the lip. Beneath that lip, on the bed, there were two men with their heads down, running.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:265:It was a left hand. Teague came up after it, a step at a time, hauling, with the right arm carried on its slat against his breast like something in a sling. He had the axe in his belt still, and had not drawn it. He got one knee onto the shelf, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:347:She did it on the bare bed, with the flat on her back, and she put out her left hand, in its linen, and laid it on his shoulder. She did not say anything, and she did not take it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-55.md:361:They carried the blanket out by the eastern mouth, the tall man and Brom, with one good arm apiece.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:13:He had not known how much noise a plain makes until he was off it. There had been grit under his boots for two days, and the rattle of shale; here there was a small dry sound at each step, and nothing came back from it. The floor was as smooth as the lid of a box, and it had no joint in it, and no bed; he had put his weight on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day, and had found that it was all one piece, and it was the same under him now for as far as he could have walked in any direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:"Then give me something," said Brom. "I've stood here an hour with my arm in a rag, watching a boy get smaller. I'll not go to my blanket on nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:241:The old man had been listening from his box. He had sat upon it since noon, when a healer told him to, and nobody had told him since that he might get down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:243:[[TIER-B: "Twenty-nine tomorrow," / "Thirty's the month," — the packet sets the two lines directly after "gives them the count instead" and pins neither speaker. Staged here: at the western stump, after Cael's count of strides, with Vastin on his box and Oryn two paces off; the first reads as the old man's own count of his days (or as Cael's, who has kept it for him in the log) and the second as the healer's answer. The paragraph above and the one below stand for either — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:249:Vastin did not get down from the box when the others went to the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:305:There was the case. It lay against the box, by his sound leg: a cord, and a mallet, and one stake that had never been driven. He had taken it up at a fork-stone, unasked, when nobody else had a hand for it; nobody had told him to, and nobody had taken it from him since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:329:"You've sat on that box since noon," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:339:"You're relieved," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-59.md:343:He got down. It took him three movements, and a thick forearm was there for the second of them, unasked, and was gone again before he could have qualified it. He took up the tarred case by its strap. He went along the rim to the fire, on the ash, at his own beat; and behind him the cut lay where it had been drawn, with nobody on either side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:She had walked on east for three reasons, and she said them over. There had been a shoulder that wanted a week of her eye, and the week was out. There had been a knee on her linen; and the knee had sat in a stream-bed today, because she had told it to, and was no worse. The third was an edge. She had said in a loft, on the far side of a cold term, where a person of her trade ought to be standing when a crew walked over such a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:75:The lean old woman said nothing. Not one word had come from her to any of the six since a cold term began, and Oryn had long since given up wondering whether it was a rule or only a habit. She had a strip of linen in her hands, and it was steaming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:101:He did it by turning onto his right side first, and then onto his knees, as he had seen an old man do it for a month; and by the time that he was on his feet, and round the rock again, the fire was a red eye. Beyond it there was a lamp burning on the lid of the middle box, with a small pale woman bent over it, and Brom sitting by with his bound arm across his lap, since he had not yet found the way to lie on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:115:She took the tarred case from beside the old man, who slept sitting against a box with his mouth a little open, and she drew out the cord herself, and the slip of ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:117:They did it on the flat of the rock, by the lamp, and they did it as it had been done at Lowmarch: one length, and four knots, and two strides. Brom held the brass eye down on a white stone with his boot. Karis ran the cord out to its fourth knot, and Brom came up; she ran it again, and laid the slip down twice. She set a second stone where it ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:"Forty-two," she said. "Brom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:127:It was tarred hemp, no thicker than a pencil, and it lay in his fingers like anything else; he had not known that he remembered the feel of it. He set the eye under Brom's boot, and ran it out to the fourth knot, and marked; and to the fourth again. Then he laid the slip of ash from his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:137:"Forty-one," said Brom slowly. "It was forty-one and a bit, in the yard. I stood there. You could lay half the slip again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:141:She pricked the lid of the box with a needle at either end of the Ternhall brass, holding it herself, and did the same for Hesk's, a hand lower down. Then she gave them to him, one after the other, and bent with the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:153:"It's more," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:*Three hundred and sixth day. The open rock east of the gap; by lamp; he had been hurt that morning. The same cord, the same two edges, the same hand. Cord: a stride short in forty-two, where it was a piece. Edges: a hair and a half; two. True in mine, and in Brom's. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*

